@@ -0,0 +1,150 @@
+# Codex automatic approval plugin bridge
+
+The local desktop installation is 3.14.4. This patch is based on source commit
+29628c9acdb81b703bbd4080c207a0e7ce5e276e (3.14.3). The independent desktop
+distribution retains the installed Electron executable and native assets, and
+replaces the Agent and desktop JavaScript with source-built bundles. This mixed patch-level compatibility must be
+tested and identified in the artifact metadata.
+
+## Ownership and flow
+
+```mermaid
+sequenceDiagram
+  participant R as Session runtime
+  participant H as Configured permission hook
+  participant P as Plugin process
+  participant M as Native model adapter
+  R->>H: PermissionRequest with exact tool input
+  H->>R: Obtain approval lease and authoritative context
+  H->>P: stdin + event-scoped bridge socket/token
+  P->>H: context/read
+  P->>H: model/complete with reviewer messages and read tool schemas
+  H->>M: Bound current model, credentials remain in host
+  M-->>P: Normalized text/tool calls
+  P-->>H: allow / deny / ask + assessment
+  H->>R: Validate lease, update per-turn denial state
+  R->>R: Execute once, return denial feedback, or prompt human
+```
+
+The runtime owns authoritative human input, session rejection action history, and per-turn counters.
+The configured hook owns its transient lease; the execution adapter owns socket
+I/O. A bridge exists only for the enabled `codex-auto-approval` plugin's foreground
+PermissionRequest process. No alternate model, provider, tools, shell, execution,
+or credential retrieval capability is exposed. Standard permission checking runs
+before and after hooks; hard denies and plan restrictions retain precedence.
+
+## Contract
+
+Bridge version 1 uses bounded NDJSON requests `{ id, token, method, params }` and
+responses `{ id, result }` or `{ id, error: { code, message, retryable } }`.
+`context/read` returns binding, complete action, current model selection, trusted
+human input records, limited visible evidence, previous denials, and deadline.
+`model/complete` accepts reviewer messages and the fixed `read_file/list_files`
+schemas and returns the existing Reviewer completion shape. Native model
+credentials are not returned. Every request is checked against the active lease.
+
+`ZCODE_APPROVAL_BRIDGE_SOCKET` and `ZCODE_APPROVAL_BRIDGE_TOKEN` are ephemeral
+child-process environment overlays. They are minted by the adapter, never
+accepted from inherited environment or project configuration, and never persisted
+or sent to the model. Windows uses named pipes; other platforms use private Unix
+sockets. `ZCODE_APPROVAL_BRIDGE_VERSION=1` advertises this contract. Missing bridge,
+invalid input, missing authoritative context, and transport failures request
+human approval, never allow.
+
+PermissionRequest gains `behavior: ask` and optional `assessment` and `bindingId`
+fields. Ask dominates automatic allow; deny dominates ask. A deny with
+`interrupt: true` stops the turn after delivering the denial tool result.
+Only a validated Codex plugin result contributes to per-turn counters.
+
+Cancellation ends the lease and native model request. Model selection, workspace,
+branch or human authorization changes invalidate the result. Stale results ask
+for a new review. An allow grants only the current unchanged tool call and never
+updates permission rules. On turn change denial counters reset; rejected actions remain in history. Policy
+denials trip at 3 consecutive or 10 among the last 50 reviews; technical errors
+are not policy denials. Plugin toggles take effect in new sessions.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+The assessment schema is shared in the contracts hook module. Permission error
+assembly owns the optional breaker turn-control result, so the permission flow
+does not duplicate error envelopes or exceed the existing module size boundary.
+
+The approval sidecar uses `ModelRetryBudget.SingleAttempt` for each native completion.
+The shared reviewer owns the outer three-attempt budget, including snapshot restarts;
+the adapter also counts initial attempts by request id. This prevents the native default
+eleven-attempt retry policy from multiplying the approval budget. Native authentication
+and runtime header refresh remain in the host.
+
+The reviewer supplies an explicit per-request output budget: at most 8192 tokens,
+clamped to the current native model's advertised maximum. Native ModelFactory
+deliberately binds only the selected reasoning level; omitting an output budget
+fails before transport. Review must preserve that bound reasoning level. Tests
+use the real native Model request validator, not only a permissive model mock.
+
+Cover real hook subprocess allow/deny/ask/cancel, malformed responses, wrong-token
+and stale leases, parallel sessions, model switching with inherited options,
+read-only investigation, policy feedback, turn-scoped breaker, hard permission
+rules, offline artifact installation, disable/uninstall and desktop startup.
+Keep the original independent CLI and its 52 tests compatible. Record actual
+checks and unverified remote-workspace support without claiming complete Codex
+equivalence. No database migration, public publication or global auth change.
+
+## CodexAutoApproval permission entry
+
+The local Windows permission menu adds a fourth radio option named exactly
+`CodexAutoApproval`; the marketplace listing has the same display name. The
+stable lowercase technical plugin id remains
+`codex-auto-approval@codex-auto-review-local` for upgrade compatibility.
+No new Agent permission enum or unimplemented `auto` mode is introduced.
+The regular Chinese subtitle is exactly `由Codex迁移的自动审批`.
+The existing new-session-required message takes precedence while enabling is blocked.
+
+The native plugin configuration owns enabled state. The existing plugin service
+and plugin management store own mutations and projection; the composer owns
+only its native permission draft. Selecting CodexAutoApproval first enables the
+plugin at workspace scope, waits for the successful native RPC/list refresh,
+then selects native `build`. Selecting any native permission first disables the
+plugin at workspace scope, then changes the native permission draft. Plan is an
+independent constraint and does not toggle the plugin. A previously enabled
+plugin aligns the idle composer with build. Workspace overrides take precedence
+over the user default; other workspaces retain their own effective settings.
+
+```mermaid
+sequenceDiagram
+  participant U as Permission menu
+  participant S as Plugin management store
+  participant H as Native plugin service/config
+  participant C as Composer draft owner
+  U->>S: Select option, workspace-scoped enabled command
+  S->>H: plugins/setEnabled
+  H-->>S: Persisted plugin result + refreshed effective listing
+  S-->>U: Success for the same workspace context
+  U->>C: build or selected native permission
+```
+
+Switching is locked while a workspace task is running or a toggle is pending.
+On RPC failure the existing store restores the projection, displays the error,
+and no native permission change is admitted. A stale workspace response cannot
+change the new workspace draft. Opening the menu refreshes the effective plugin
+state. The entry is hidden for non-Windows, browser and remote workspaces, and
+when this plugin is not installed. Keyboard cycling follows the same command
+path. Installed/enabled at user scope remains a default for new sessions;
+plugin enable is admitted only from a new-session composer. An existing session
+that has no enabled plugin cannot hot-load its frozen Hook catalog; its option
+is disabled with a new-session explanation. Disable remains available for idle
+existing sessions, and the activity bridge cancels/restores native approval.
+The bridge activity check merges user then project enable flags with the same
+last-defined precedence as native config; workspace enable can override user
+disable and must not be rejected by an independent any-false check.
+
+Desktop acceptance must verify four distinct radio entries, exact display name
+in the plugin settings, keyboard enable, native-mode disable with persisted
+workspace config, Plan independence, restored automatic review, original denial
+and failure behavior, and uninstall restoring native approval. Repack only an
+independent desktop copy; preserve the original installation and active app.
+
+The adapted production desktop uses `ZCode AutoReview` as its default runtime
+application identity. This preserves the existing adapted userData directory,
+avoids the official app's single-instance lock, and makes `ZCode.exe` directly
+launchable without a cmd environment wrapper. Development and Preview defaults,
+explicit test overrides and shared native user plugin settings stay unchanged.
